---
// src/pages/brands/true-residential.astro
// True Residential brand pillar (Tier 1, brand-pillar wave 2026-07-17).
// Spine of the page = the commercial/residential disambiguation. Every factual
// claim is sourced; see the source log below and the session log.
//
// SOURCE LOG (facts that must not drift):
//  - OWNERSHIP, SETTLED: True Residential is the residential line of True
//    Manufacturing Co., Inc. — Trulaske family, O'Fallon MO, family-owned since
//    1945. It is NOT a Middleby company and never was.
//    Proof: True Residential warranty claims go to "True Residential, 2001 East
//    Terra Lane, O'Fallon, MO 63366, TrueResidentialClaims@TrueMfg.com" — the
//    same address and domain as True Manufacturing.
//    [true-caliber.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/True-Residential-Warranty-05-2024.pdf]
//    Middleby's FY2024 10-K lists 24 residential brands; True is NOT among them.
//    [sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/769520/000076952025000009/midd-20241228.htm]
//    Middleby OWNED U-Line and Marvel — premium undercounter competitors in the
//    same category. That is almost certainly the source of the confusion in
//    docs/factual-accuracy.md §1 (FIXED 2026-07-17, see §1 + new §1.1).
//    As of 2026-02-02 U-Line and Marvel are COMPOSITION BRANDS, not Middleby:
//    Middleby 10-K FY2026 — "sell a 51% stake in its Residential Kitchen Equipment
//    Group to an affiliate of 26North Partners LP ... valuing the business at
//    $885 million ... completed on February 2, 2026 ... owns a 49% non-controlling
//    interest". sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/769520/000076952026000011/midd-20260103.htm
//    Rebrand name is NOT in SEC filings; primary = 26North release
//    businesswire.com/news/home/20260202412606/en/ ; roster compositionbrands.com/our-brands/
//  - Residential line launched 2008 (previously "True Professional Series").
//    VERIFIED on the official heritage timeline (true-residential.com/heritage ->
//    301 true-caliber.com/heritage): "2008 - True Professional Series officially
//    launches first generation product line in the California market."
//    NOT 2006 — 2006 in the same timeline is a COMMERCIAL expansion; that is where
//    our old combo page got 2006 from. Launch was regional (California), so do not
//    write "launched nationwide in 2008".
//  - Feb 2025: True partnered with grill brand Caliber -> combined brand
//    "True Caliber"; true-residential.com now 301s to true-caliber.com. This is
//    a PARTNERSHIP, not a change of ownership — Trulaske retains True Mfg.
//    [globenewswire 2025/02/25/3032133]
//
//  - SIZES (the brief was wrong; do not "correct" this back):
//    Refrigerator/freezer COLUMNS = 24" / 30" / 36" ONLY.
//    42" and 48" are SIDE-BY-SIDE refrigerators, NOT columns.
//    Undercounter = 15" and 24". Ice machines = 15" clear ice.
//    [true-caliber.com/products/ , /products/the-true-42/ , /the-true-48/]
//
//  - ERROR CODES (the trap): residential True has NO alphanumeric display fault
//    table. E1 / E2 / P2 / P3 belong to True COMMERCIAL merchandisers (GDM-26,
//    T-49) — putting them on this page would be the exact error this page cures.
//    What residential actually has: software version codes shown at power-up
//    (66 = A-versions, 73 = older B, 94 = current B — these are versions, NOT
//    faults), and an inverter-board LED flash table.
//    [true-caliber.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/15__24_Inch_Service_Manual_for_Web.pdf]
//  - Compressors Embraco, R-134A. Variable-speed units: 3-phase DC + inverter
//    board, 4500-1600 rpm. "Applying line voltage to compressor will damage it."
//    Anti-short-cycle preset 7 min; 2-min start delay; dedicated 15A circuit.
//  - "Cut-out temperature is based on the compartment probe, NOT the display
//    temperature. Our display temperature is a calculation and is not reflecting
//    real time temp." — direct from the service manual.
//  - Warranty: 30d cosmetic; 3yr parts+labour (max 39mo from shipment); 6yr
//    sealed system parts+labour; years 7-12 sealed system PARTS ONLY, requires
//    registration within 12mo, shipments after 1 Feb 2017, excludes factory
//    seconds / ADA / clear-ice machines. NOT TRANSFERABLE. Unauthorised repair
//    voids it. [warranty PDF above]
//  - Service channels (all @truemfg.com): Residential Service 844-746-9423;
//    Warranty 844-849-6179; Parts 844-849-6226. Commercial is a DIFFERENT line:
//    800-325-6152. Service locator: true-caliber.com/service-locator/
//
// DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED (research returned NOT VERIFIED):
//  - UL rating / outdoor rating on residential units — do not assert.
//  - Commercial "rack design" comparison — do not assert.
//  - Do NOT publish E1/E2/P2/P3 on this page under any circumstance.
//
// No fabricated repair records — same rule as la-cornue.astro. We have no True
// Residential job history; the diagnostic section describes mechanics instead.

import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../data/branches';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/brands/true-residential/";

const title = "True Residential Appliance Repair Los Angeles — Same Day";
const description = "True Residential column, undercounter & wine refrigeration repair in LA. Not the commercial T-49 — different unit, different codes. $89 diagnostic, waived with repair.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is True Residential the same company as the True commercial fridges in restaurants?",
    a: "Same company, different product — and getting this wrong is the single most common mistake we see. <strong>True Residential is the residential line of True Manufacturing</strong>, the Trulaske family business in O'Fallon, Missouri, family-owned since 1945. The residential line launched in 2008. It is not a separate company, and it is <strong>not</strong> owned by Middleby — a claim that circulates widely and is simply false. The giveaway is the paperwork: True Residential warranty claims go to True Residential, 2001 East Terra Lane, O'Fallon, MO — the same address and the same <code>truemfg.com</code> domain as the commercial side. Where the confusion comes from is that Middleby <em>owned</em> U-Line and Marvel — premium undercounter brands competing in exactly this category. (Those two left Middleby on 2 February 2026, when 26North Partners bought 51% of Middleby's residential kitchen business and renamed it Composition Brands; Middleby kept a 49% non-controlling stake.) Different companies from True either way. So: same manufacturer as the restaurant units, deliberately different machine — and if you call the commercial service line about your kitchen column, you'll get routed to the wrong department."
  },
  {
    q: "What error code is my True Residential showing?",
    a: "Probably none — and if someone quoted you an <code>E1</code> or <code>E2</code>, they were reading a commercial manual. <strong>True Residential units have no alphanumeric fault-code table on the display.</strong> E1 / E2 / P2 / P3 belong to True <em>commercial</em> merchandisers like the GDM-26 and T-49. What residential units actually show is a <strong>software version number at power-up</strong> — 66 on A-versions, 73 on older B-versions, 94 on current B-versions, displayed for about 25 seconds when power is applied. That's a version, not a fault. The real diagnostic table on these units is the <strong>inverter board's LED flash pattern</strong>, which you read at the board, not the display: one flash every 15 seconds is normal running; two flashes per five seconds is a control-communication problem; three is an inverter hardware fault; four is a compressor fault. So when a page hands you a True error-code table, check whether it's describing your kitchen or a restaurant."
  },
  {
    q: "What sizes does True Residential actually make?",
    a: "Worth being precise, because the terminology gets mangled constantly. <strong>Columns come in 24, 30 and 36 inches</strong> — refrigerator columns, freezer columns, wine columns and beverage columns. <strong>42-inch and 48-inch True units are side-by-side refrigerators, not columns.</strong> If a company offers you a \"42-inch column,\" they don't know the product. Undercounter units — refrigerators, freezers, wine, beverage, drawers — are <strong>15 and 24 inches</strong>, with ADA low-profile variants at 24. Bottom-freezer refrigerators come at 30 and 36. There are also <strong>15-inch clear-ice machines</strong>. Tell us the size and the category when you call and we'll know which manual we're in before we arrive."
  },
  {
    q: "My True Residential display says 38° but the food is warm. Is the display lying?",
    a: "Sort of, and True says so themselves. From the service manual, verbatim in spirit: <strong>the cut-out temperature is based on the compartment probe, not the display temperature — the display temperature is a calculation and does not reflect real-time temp.</strong> That single line explains a huge share of \"my True is reading fine but it isn't cold\" calls. The control is acting on what the compartment probe reports; the number on the front is a smoothed calculation for your benefit. So our techs don't diagnose off the display. We put an independent probe in the compartment, read the compartment and evaporator probes directly, and compare. If the probe is drifting, the control is making correct decisions from wrong information — and the display will happily keep showing you a comfortable number the whole time."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the True Residential warranty, and will a repair void it?",
    a: "The structure, from True's own warranty document: <strong>30 days</strong> on cosmetics (stainless doors, handles, shelves); <strong>3 years parts and labour</strong> on the cabinet and all parts from installation (capped at 39 months from shipment); <strong>6 years parts and labour</strong> on the sealed system — compressor, evaporator, condenser, filter-drier, expansion valve, tubing; and <strong>years 7 through 12 on the sealed system, parts only</strong>, which requires you to have registered the product within 12 months of installation and applies to units shipped after 1 February 2017. Factory seconds, ADA models and clear-ice machines are excluded from that last tier. Two things that catch people out: <strong>the warranty is not transferable</strong> — if the unit came with the house, you have no warranty, full stop — and <strong>unauthorised repair voids it</strong>. So if you're inside coverage, call True's residential line at 844-746-9423 and use their channel. We'll tell you that on the phone rather than take the job and cost you six years of sealed-system cover."
  },
  {
    q: "Are you a True Residential authorized service company?",
    a: "<strong>No, and you should know that before you book.</strong> True publishes a Service Company Locator and trains outside field-service providers, so their network isn't closed — but warranty claims are processed by True, and a repair performed outside that process terminates your coverage. If your unit is still covered, use their people. The residential numbers are service <strong>844-746-9423</strong>, warranty 844-849-6179, parts 844-849-6226. Watch which number you dial: the commercial department is 800-325-6152 and handles the restaurant equipment, and they will hand you back. An independent is the right answer once coverage has lapsed, or when it never applied to you in the first place because the unit came with the house. BHGS #A49573."
  },
  {
    q: "Does True Residential make ranges or dishwashers?",
    a: "No. <strong>True Residential is refrigeration</strong> — columns, side-by-sides, undercounter units, wine cabinets, beverage centres, drawers and clear-ice machines. No ranges, no ovens, no cooktops, no dishwashers, no laundry. The one non-refrigeration category is grills and griddles, and that's recent: in February 2025 True partnered with the grill brand <strong>Caliber</strong>, producing the combined <strong>True Caliber</strong> brand, with True as the manufacturer of Caliber grills. That's a partnership, not a sale — the Trulaske family still owns True Manufacturing. Practical note if you're looking for the brand online: <code>true-residential.com</code> now redirects to <code>true-caliber.com</code>."
  },
  {
    q: "How much does True Residential repair cost in Los Angeles?",
    a: `The diagnostic is <strong>$89</strong> and it comes off the bill when you approve the repair. Past that we quote per job. The honest reason we don't publish a rate card for this brand: the spread between a door gasket and a variable-speed inverter board is wide, and a "typical price" would mislead most people reading it. What we commit to is the part price and the labour before we start, and the invoice matching the quote. Call ${phone} with the model and serial off the plate — and tell us whether it's a column, a side-by-side, an undercounter or a wine cabinet, because that determines which service manual and which parts we bring.`
  }
];

const schemaJsons = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Service",
    "@id": canonical + "#service",
    "serviceType": "True Residential Refrigerator Repair",
    "name": "True Residential Refrigeration Repair, Same Day Appliance Repair",
    "url": canonical,
    "description": "Same-day True Residential refrigeration repair across Los Angeles, Orange, Ventura, Santa Barbara, San Bernardino, and Riverside counties. Refrigerator, freezer, wine and beverage columns (24/30/36 inch), 42 and 48 inch side-by-side refrigerators, 15 and 24 inch undercounter units, drawers, and 15 inch clear-ice machines. True Residential is the residential line of True Manufacturing (Trulaske family, O'Fallon Missouri, family-owned since 1945) — a different machine from the True commercial merchandisers, with different controls and no E1/E2 commercial fault codes. Embraco compressors, R-134A, variable-speed inverter systems. Independent service, not factory-authorized. $89 residential diagnostic, applied toward repair.",
    "provider": {
      "@type": "LocalBusiness",
      "address": { "@type": "PostalAddress", "addressLocality": "West Hollywood", "addressRegion": "CA", "addressCountry": "US" },
      "name": "Same Day Appliance Repair",
      "legalName": "HVAC 777 LLC",
      "url": "https://samedayappliance.repair/",
      "telephone": phone,
      "priceRange": "$$$",
      "openingHoursSpecification": [
        { "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification", "dayOfWeek": ["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday"], "opens": "08:00", "closes": "20:00" },
        { "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification", "dayOfWeek": "Sunday", "opens": "00:00", "closes": "00:00" }
      ],
      "hasCredential": [
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "BHGS Registration #A49573", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "California Bureau of Household Goods and Services" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "EPA 608 Universal Certification #1346255700410", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "U.S. Environmental Protection Agency" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "CSLB C-20 HVAC", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "California Contractors State License Board" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "BBB Accredited Business", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Better Business Bureau" } }
      ]
    },
    "areaServed": [
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Los Angeles County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Orange County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Ventura County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Santa Barbara County" }, { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "San Bernardino County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Riverside County" }
    ],
    "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "True Residential", "sameAs": "https://true-caliber.com/" },
    "offers": {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "price": "89.00",
      "priceCurrency": "USD",
      "description": "Residential diagnostic fee, waived when the repair is approved."
    }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "@id": canonical + "#faq",
    "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": f.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '') }
    }))
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "@id": canonical + "#breadcrumb",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://samedayappliance.repair/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Brands", "item": "https://samedayappliance.repair/brands/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "True Residential", "item": canonical }
    ]
  }
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head-scripts">
    {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
  </Fragment>

  <ServiceHero
    title={"True Residential Appliance Repair Los Angeles"}
    subtitle={"Columns, side-by-sides, undercounter and wine. The residential line of True Manufacturing — not the commercial T-49, and not a Middleby brand. $89 diagnostic, waived with the repair."}
    heroImageTitle="True Residential"
  />

  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span class="crumbs-sep">›</span>
    <a href="/brands/">Brands</a> <span class="crumbs-sep">›</span>
    <span aria-current="page">True Residential</span>
  </nav>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">01, which True is this</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Half the pages you'll find about True refrigerator repair are describing a restaurant merchandiser — not the column in your kitchen.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>This is the brand where nearly everyone, including some repair companies, gets the basics wrong. So, plainly:</p>
        <p><strong>True Residential is the residential line of True Manufacturing.</strong> Same company. The Trulaske family started True in O'Fallon, Missouri in 1945 and still own it — four generations in. The residential line launched in 2008, having previously been sold as the True Professional Series. It is <strong>not</strong> a separate company, and it is <strong>not</strong> owned by Middleby, which is the most persistent piece of misinformation about this brand.</p>
        <p>If you want to settle that yourself, look at the warranty document: True Residential claims go to <strong>True Residential, 2001 East Terra Lane, O'Fallon, MO</strong>, at a <code>truemfg.com</code> address. Same building, same domain, same family as the commercial side. Middleby's own annual filing lists its residential brands, and True isn't among them. What Middleby <em>did</em> own is <strong>U-Line</strong> and <strong>Marvel</strong> — premium undercounter refrigeration, competing directly with True Residential in the same category. That's almost certainly where the mix-up started, and it's an easy one to make. (Both left Middleby on 2 February 2026, when 26North Partners took 51% of Middleby's residential kitchen business and the group was renamed Composition Brands — so "Middleby owns U-Line" is now stale too.)</p>
        <p>Why it matters to you at 8am with a warm column: <strong>the commercial and residential units are different machines with different controls.</strong> If you ring the commercial line you'll be routed back. If you're handed an E1/E2 fault-code table, it's for a merchandiser. And if a company shows up expecting a T-49, they'll be reading the wrong manual in your kitchen.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">02, the code trap</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">E1 and E2 are commercial codes. Your kitchen unit doesn't have them.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Search "True refrigerator error code" and you'll get a tidy table of <code>E1</code>, <code>E2</code>, <code>P2</code>, <code>P3</code>. Those are real — for True <em>commercial</em> merchandisers, the GDM-26s and T-49s behind a bar. <strong>Residential True units have no alphanumeric fault-code table on the display.</strong> Anyone who reads you one is reading the wrong document.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>What the number at power-up actually is</h3>
          <p>Apply power and the display shows a number for roughly 25 seconds: <strong>66</strong> on A-versions, <strong>73</strong> on older B-versions, <strong>94</strong> on current B-versions. That's the <strong>software version</strong>, not a fault. People report it to us as an error constantly. It isn't one — it's the unit telling you which firmware it's running, which is genuinely useful to us before we drive out.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>The real diagnostic table: inverter LED flashes</h3>
          <p>On variable-speed units the honest fault indicator is the <strong>inverter board LED</strong>, read at the board rather than the front panel. One flash every 15 seconds is normal operation. <strong>Two flashes</strong> per five seconds is a control-communication problem. <strong>Three</strong> is an inverter hardware fault. <strong>Four</strong> is a compressor fault — failure to hold above 1600 rpm under overload, a start failure, a rotor-position failure, or the inverter cooking itself. A single flash on its own is temperature protection, the inverter throttling because it's too hot.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>The display is a calculation, not a thermometer</h3>
          <p>Straight from True's service manual: the cut-out temperature is based on the <strong>compartment probe, not the display</strong> — and the display temperature is a calculation that does not reflect real-time temp. This explains most "it reads 38 but the milk's warm" calls. The control acts on the probe. The front panel shows you a smoothed number. Our techs read the probes, not the panel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Timings that look like faults</h3>
          <p>Anti-short-cycle protection is preset to <strong>7 minutes</strong> and the refrigeration system waits about <strong>2 minutes</strong> after power-up before starting. Both regularly get reported as "it won't turn on." Give it the timer before you give it a diagnosis.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">03, sizes</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Columns are 24, 30 and 36. The 42 and 48 are side-by-sides — and the difference isn't pedantry.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The terminology on this brand gets mangled everywhere, including by companies quoting you for work. Getting it right determines which manual we open and which parts go on the van.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Columns — 24″ / 30″ / 36″</h3>
          <p>Refrigerator columns, freezer columns, wine columns, beverage columns. These are the integrated, panel-ready units in a run of cabinetry. <strong>There is no such thing as a 42-inch True column.</strong> If you've been offered one, the company doesn't know the product.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Side-by-sides — 42″ and 48″</h3>
          <p>The True 42 and True 48, in glass or solid door, running a forced-air refrigeration system. These are where the 42/48 numbers actually belong.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Undercounter — 15″ and 24″</h3>
          <p>Refrigerators, freezers, wine, beverage centres and drawers, plus ADA low-profile variants at 24 inches. The bar and butler's-pantry units, and the bulk of what we see in Westside builds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Wine, beverage and ice</h3>
          <p>Wine columns at 24/30/36 and undercounter wine at 15/24; beverage columns at 24/30; and <strong>15-inch clear-ice machines</strong>. Minimum set points are <strong>33°F on refrigerators, 40°F on wine</strong> — worth knowing before deciding something is "not cold enough."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Dual-zone wine</h3>
          <p>Dual-zone cabinets run two genuinely independent zones — each with its own display, evaporator, fan and sensors. One zone failing while the other is perfect isn't a mystery; it's two systems in one box.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Bottom-freezer</h3>
          <p>30 and 36 inch. Conventional layout, True build.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:28px;">
        <p>A practical note on the clear-ice machines, because they're the units people most often assume are broken when they aren't. Clear ice is made slowly and deliberately — water is frozen in layers so the air comes out of it, which is what makes the cube optically clear instead of cloudy. That process is meant to take hours, and the harvest cycle looks alarming if you've never watched one. A machine producing less ice than a hotel dispenser is not a fault; it's the design. What <em>is</em> a fault is no harvest at all, a bin that never fills, or water that freezes in the line. Also worth knowing before you register anything: the <strong>clear-ice machines are excluded from the extended sealed-system tier</strong> that covers years seven through twelve on the refrigeration, so their warranty maths is different from the column next to them.</p>
        <p>And on dual-zone wine cabinets specifically, one thing spares a lot of confusion: the two zones are genuinely separate systems in one cabinet — separate displays, separate evaporators, separate fans, separate sensors. When the top zone is perfect and the bottom is warm, that is not a partial failure of one machine; it's one of two machines failing. It also means the repair is scoped to a zone, which usually makes it cheaper than people fear when they call.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">04, what actually fails</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Variable-speed inverter refrigeration — where these units actually go wrong.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Same discipline as the rest of this site's newer pages: no invented job records. We don't have a True Residential service history to quote at you, and making one up to fill the page would be worse than leaving it out. Here's the mechanical reality instead.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>The compressor is not a normal compressor</h3>
          <p>These run <strong>Embraco</strong> compressors on <strong>R-134A</strong>. The variable-speed units use a three-phase DC compressor driven by an inverter board, modulating roughly <strong>4500 down to 1600 rpm</strong>. The manual is blunt about it: <strong>applying line voltage to the compressor will damage it.</strong> That's the single most important thing to know about a True Residential, and it's why a generalist who treats it like a standard single-speed can turn a board fault into a compressor replacement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Single-speed vs variable-speed is not interchangeable</h3>
          <p>Some 24-inch B-versions are single speed; the 15-inch B-versions are variable. True's own guidance is not to convert between the two in either direction. Which one you have is determined by the serial plate and the software version — another reason we ask for both on the phone.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Probes vs display</h3>
          <p>Two sensors do the work: a compartment probe driving cut-in/cut-out, and an evaporator probe managing defrost. Because the display is a calculation rather than a live reading, a drifting compartment probe produces a unit making perfectly logical decisions from bad data — while the panel reassures you. Diagnosis is at the probes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Power quality</h3>
          <p>True asks for a <strong>dedicated, grounded 15-amp circuit</strong>, and their warranty explicitly excludes failures caused by voltage surges, poor grounding, and <strong>extension cords</strong>. On inverter-driven refrigeration this isn't fine print — the inverter is the expensive thing and it's the thing that dies. If a wine cabinet is on an extension lead behind a bar, that's a finding, not a footnote.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Evaporator fan behaviour</h3>
          <p>On refrigerators the evaporator fan cycles with the system; <strong>on wine units it runs continuously</strong>; on both it stops when the door opens. A wine cabinet whose fan never stops is behaving correctly, and we'd rather tell you that than sell you a fan.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>LED colour drift</h3>
          <p>The interior LEDs are 10V DC modules with an IC chip, and the colour can drift out of sync between modules. There's a documented reset — cycling the colour ten times in a row returns them to white. Not everything is a part.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">05, warranty and the factory channel</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">If you're in warranty, use True's channel — and know that it didn't come with the house.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The structure, from True's own document. <strong>30 days</strong> on cosmetics. <strong>3 years parts and labour</strong> on the cabinet and all parts from installation, capped at 39 months from shipment. <strong>6 years parts and labour</strong> on the sealed system — compressor, evaporator, condenser, filter-drier, expansion valve, tubing. Then <strong>years 7 to 12 on the sealed system, parts only</strong>, which requires registration within 12 months of installation and applies to units shipped after 1 February 2017; factory seconds, ADA models and clear-ice machines are excluded from that tier.</p>
        <p>Two clauses catch people out, and both are worth knowing before you call anyone. <strong>The warranty is not transferable.</strong> If the unit came with the house, you have no warranty regardless of the unit's age — transferring it voids it outright. And <strong>unauthorised repair voids coverage</strong>. So if you're inside the window, call True's residential service line at <strong>844-746-9423</strong> (warranty 844-849-6179, parts 844-849-6226) and use their people. Note those are the residential numbers — the commercial line is 800-325-6152 and is a different department that will send you back.</p>
        <p>Our own position on that: we are not on True's authorized list, and you should hear it from us rather than discover it later. True's network isn't a walled garden — they publish a Service Company Locator and they train outside field-service providers — but the claims themselves route through O'Fallon, and a repair by anyone outside that process ends your coverage. So the decision tree is simple. In warranty, registered, sealed-system fault? Call True. Out of warranty, or the unit arrived with the deed and the warranty died at closing — which on Westside property is the normal case, not the exception — that's where an independent earns the work. BHGS #A49573, and the invoice carries our name, not a dispatcher's.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">06, scope</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">True Residential is refrigeration. The grills are a 2025 development.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Columns, side-by-sides, undercounter units, wine cabinets, beverage centres, drawers, clear-ice machines. <strong>No ranges, no ovens, no cooktops, no dishwashers, no laundry.</strong> If your True kitchen has a range in it, the range is another brand.</p>
        <p>The one exception is recent and worth knowing about, because it changes what you'll find when you search. In <strong>February 2025 True partnered with the grill brand Caliber</strong>, becoming the manufacturer of Caliber grills under a combined <strong>True Caliber</strong> brand. That's a partnership, not an acquisition of True — the Trulaske family still own True Manufacturing. Practical consequence: <code>true-residential.com</code> now redirects to <code>true-caliber.com</code>, which surprises people looking for the brand they bought.</p>
        <p>For the commercial side — reach-ins, merchandisers, back-bar and prep units, the T-49s and GDM-26s with the actual E-codes — that's a different machine and a different fee. See our <a href="/brands/true/">True commercial refrigeration page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">07, the category</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Where True Residential sits — and what it's usually specified against.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Understanding the competitive set helps, because it explains why your kitchen has what it has. Premium built-in refrigeration in this class comes down to a short list: Sub-Zero, which invented the category and dominates it; <a href="/brands/true-residential/">True Residential</a>; <a href="/brands/u-line/">U-Line</a> and <a href="/brands/marvel/">Marvel</a>, both of which moved from Middleby to Composition Brands in February 2026; <a href="/brands/liebherr/">Liebherr</a> from Germany; and <a href="/brands/perlick-refrigerator-repair/">Perlick</a>, the independent Wisconsin family firm. True's pitch inside that set is its parentage: the residential units come out of a company that has spent eight decades building refrigeration for commercial kitchens, where a failure costs a restaurant its night. That heritage is the argument, and it's a real one.</p>
        <p>It also explains the design decisions that surprise people. The <strong>7-minute anti-short-cycle lockout</strong> and the <strong>2-minute start delay</strong> are commercial habits — protect the compressor, always, even when it makes the machine look unresponsive. The dedicated 15-amp circuit requirement is a commercial habit. The forced-air system in the 42 and 48 side-by-sides is a commercial habit. None of that is a defect; it's a restaurant company building a home appliance and not pretending otherwise.</p>
        <p>The flip side is worth saying plainly: a True Residential is not a Sub-Zero and doesn't fail like one. The Sub-Zero service literature, the Sub-Zero parts channel, and the Sub-Zero diagnostic habits do not transfer. A tech who has spent fifteen years on Sub-Zero 600-series units and has never opened a True is going to be reading the manual in your kitchen — and on a variable-speed inverter unit, the specific way that goes wrong is expensive.</p>
        <p>Something else follows from the commercial parentage that's worth knowing if you're weighing a repair. True's own warranty language contemplates these units being installed in <strong>commercial and light-commercial settings</strong> and asks you to contact them about coverage if that's your situation — which tells you something about how the machine is built, and is a live question in Los Angeles, where the line between a large residential kitchen, a guesthouse, and a small hospitality operation is genuinely blurry. If your True is in a short-term rental, a private club, an office, or a staffed estate kitchen, your warranty position may not be what the brochure implied. Worth a call to True before you assume either way.</p>
        <p>And the age question, since we get asked. There is no useful "how long does a True Residential last" number, and we're not going to invent one — the residential line only launched in 2008, so the field data simply doesn't stretch far enough to make the kind of lifespan claim you'll see quoted for Sub-Zero. What we can say is structural: the sealed system carries six years of parts-and-labour coverage and up to twelve years of parts coverage if you registered it, and a manufacturer doesn't write those terms for a component it expects to fail early. Treat that warranty schedule as the company's own estimate of the machine, because that's exactly what it is.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">08, coverage</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">True Residential service across Greater Los Angeles.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The pattern of where these units live is distinctive. Columns and side-by-sides show up in the panel-ready kitchens — Beverly Hills, Bel Air, Brentwood, Pacific Palisades, Malibu, Manhattan Beach, and the newer Hidden Hills and Calabasas builds. The undercounter units live somewhere else entirely: bars, butler's pantries, home offices, wine rooms, pool houses. It's common for one house to have four True units in four rooms, bought at different times, running different software versions, and for only one of them to be the problem. When you call, tell us which room.</p>
        <p>Two things make the visit go faster on this brand specifically. Have the <strong>model and serial off the plate</strong>, and if you can catch it, the <strong>number the display shows in the first 25 seconds after power-up</strong>. That number is the software version — 66, 73 or 94 — and it tells us whether you're on an A-version or a B-version, which determines the compressor type, the board, and which service manual we open before we leave the shop. It's the single most useful thing a True owner can tell us on the phone, and almost nobody knows to mention it.</p>
        <p>Reach us on <a href={phoneHref}>{phone}</a>. If you're inside the Westside we can usually be there the same day; further out into Orange or Ventura we'd rather give you a straight answer about tomorrow than a window we can't hold.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

